WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:08.466
Every cyber crime has a route. A First Responder's job is to put the citizen on the right line, fast.

2
00:00:08.966 --> 00:00:18.089
Money lost online takes the red line. Call nineteen thirty first: banks get the details in real time, so the money can be put on hold.

3
00:00:18.489 --> 00:00:23.779
Then call the bank on its official number, and file on the portal within twenty-four hours.

4
00:00:24.279 --> 00:00:33.716
Cybercrime dot gov dot in takes every cyber crime, and sends it to the police of your state and district. Save the acknowledgement number.

5
00:00:34.316 --> 00:00:40.033
But the police station told me: not our area. Go somewhere else.

6
00:00:40.433 --> 00:00:48.427
That is not right. Under the BNSS, any police station must register a Zero FIR, and transfer it.

7
00:00:49.227 --> 00:01:02.547
Fake morphed photos, sextortion, or a child at risk take the purple line: the portal's women and children section. Ten ninety-eight for a child, one eight one for women, one one two in danger.

8
00:01:03.047 --> 00:01:08.345
A suspicious call or message, with no loss, goes to Chakshu on Sanchar Saathi.

9
00:01:08.845 --> 00:01:20.563
Bank not resolving it in thirty days? Go to the RBI Ombudsman. A ransomware attack at an office? Report it to CERT-In, and to the police.

10
00:01:21.063 --> 00:01:31.474
Behind every line is the law: the IT Act covers identity theft and cheating by personation. The BNS covers cheating, extortion and stalking.

11
00:01:32.074 --> 00:01:38.771
Watch out for fake helplines and paid recovery agents. Real helplines never charge a fee.

12
00:01:39.371 --> 00:01:47.119
One line to remember: money lost, nineteen thirty. Everything else, cybercrime dot gov dot in.
